.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt SOWA-SETUP 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-setup
.Nd install Sowa from the live environment onto a disk
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
takes a whole disk and does everything: partitions it, makes the filesystems,
copies the running system across and installs GRUB for both legacy BIOS and
UEFI firmware.
An interactive run asks for the partition-table format, root filesystem, NIC
configuration and root password.
Environment variables provide the same choices to an unattended run.
.Pp
Booted from the ISO, Sowa's root filesystem is a read-only squashfs on the
medium with a tmpfs overlaid on it.
What is copied is the merged view, so anything changed since boot - a password
set, a package installed - is copied as it is now.
.Pp
The default is an MBR partition table with a FAT EFI System Partition and an
ext4 root, preserving the layout used by earlier releases.
GPT can be selected instead; it adds a 1 MiB BIOS Boot Partition ahead of the
ESP.
With MBR, GRUB's BIOS core is embedded in the reserved post-MBR gap.
The UEFI image is installed to the ESP in removable mode
.Pq Pa EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI ,
so no firmware NVRAM entry is needed and no
.Xr efibootmgr 8
with it.
The installed system boots the on-disk kernel directly with
.Ar root=PARTUUID=... ,
with no initramfs between them.
.Pp
The root may be ext4, XFS or Btrfs.
All three drivers are built into the Sowa kernel, so each can be mounted as the
direct root without an initramfs.
An ext4 root is made with
.Cm orphan_file
and
.Cm metadata_csum_seed
switched off.
e2fsprogs 1.47 turns both on by default and GRUB 2.12's ext2 driver cannot read
either, so a filesystem made without this lands at a rescue prompt instead of a
boot menu.
.Pp
Before it writes anything,
.Nm
checks that the target is a whole disk rather than a partition, that none of its
partitions is mounted, that the live system has a kernel to copy, that the
selected filesystem tool is installed, and that what is left after the ESP is
large enough for the system and filesystem metadata.
Then it says what it is about to destroy and waits to be told to go ahead.
.Pp
Once the erase is confirmed and before anything is written,
.Nm
asks for the root password the installed system will have, twice, without
echoing it.
It is asked here rather than at the end because the copy that follows takes
minutes and there is nobody waiting at the end of them.
The image ships root with an empty password, which is right for a live system
that is thrown away at reboot and wrong for one being installed, so an empty
answer is accepted - a machine reached only by SSH key wants exactly that - and
said out loud rather than taken silently.
The question is skipped, and the account left as the image ships it, when
.Ev SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_PASSWORD
already answers it, when
.Ev SOWA_SETUP_ASSUME_YES
says the run is unattended, when standard input is not a terminal, or when
there is no
.Xr openssl 1
to hash the answer with.
.Pp
Host keys are not copied: a shipped or copied host key would give every
installation the same identity.
The installed system generates its own at first boot.
.Pp
NIC mode
.Cm keep
copies the running system's
.Pa /etc/nic.conf
unchanged.
The guided DHCP, static and firewall-only modes generate a replacement while
retaining Sowa's default IPv4 and IPv6 firewall rules.
A complete configuration file can be supplied instead when the guided fields
are not enough.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
is interactive by default.
For an unattended install, set these:
.Bl -tag -width SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_FILESYSTEM
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_DISK
The target device, for example
.Pa /dev/sda .
Set, and the menu is skipped.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_ASSUME_YES
Set to 1 to skip the confirmation before the disk is erased and the root
password prompt with it.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_PASSWORD
The root password for the installed system, given instead of being asked for.
Unset, an interactive run asks; an unattended one installs the account as the
image ships it, with no password at all.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_ESP_SIZE_MB
The size of the EFI System Partition in MiB.
The default is 512 and the minimum is 34, below which
.Xr mkfs.fat 8
cannot make the 65525 clusters FAT32 requires.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_HOSTNAME
The hostname for the installed system.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_PARTITION_TABLE
.Cm mbr
or
.Cm gpt .
The unattended default is
.Cm mbr .
The spelling
.Cm dos
is accepted as an alias for
.Cm mbr .
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_FILESYSTEM
.Cm ext4 ,
.Cm xfs
or
.Cm btrfs .
The unattended default is
.Cm ext4 .
The misspelling
.Cm brfs
is accepted as an alias for
.Cm btrfs .
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_CONFIG_FILE
A complete
.Cm nic
configuration to install as
.Pa /etc/nic.conf .
It is validated before the disk is written and cannot be combined with
.Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_MODE .
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_MODE
One of
.Cm keep ,
.Cm dhcp ,
.Cm static
or
.Cm firewall-only .
The unattended default is
.Cm keep .
The last mode configures no non-loopback interface, but still lets the network
service restore the default firewall.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_INTERFACE
The interface used by
.Cm dhcp
or
.Cm static .
The current default-route interface, the first non-loopback interface, or
.Cm eth0
is selected when this is unset.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_ADDRESS
The address, normally in CIDR notation, required by static mode.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_GATEWAY
An optional default gateway for static mode.
.It Ev SOWA_SETUP_NIC_DNS
Optional DNS server addresses separated by spaces or commas for static mode.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/lib/sowa/install-functions" -compact
.It Pa /usr/lib/sowa/install-functions
the copy, the host-key reset and the mount points, shared with
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8
and
.Xr sowa-chroot 8 .
.It Pa /mnt
where the target root is mounted while the copy runs.
.It Pa /boot/vmlinuz
the kernel that must be there to copy.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Every check that fails does so before anything has been written.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The whole interactive install:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-setup
.Pp
Unattended, onto a virtual disk, with a password and a name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SOWA_SETUP_DISK=/dev/vda \e
SOWA_SETUP_ASSUME_YES=1 \e
SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_PASSWORD=hunter2 \e
SOWA_SETUP_HOSTNAME=web01 \e
    sowa-setup
.Ed
.Pp
GPT, XFS and a static interface:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SOWA_SETUP_DISK=/dev/nvme0n1 \e
SOWA_SETUP_ASSUME_YES=1 \e
SOWA_SETUP_PARTITION_TABLE=gpt \e
SOWA_SETUP_ROOT_FILESYSTEM=xfs \e
SOWA_SETUP_NIC_MODE=static \e
SOWA_SETUP_NIC_INTERFACE=enp1s0 \e
SOWA_SETUP_NIC_ADDRESS=192.0.2.10/24 \e
SOWA_SETUP_NIC_GATEWAY=192.0.2.1 \e
SOWA_SETUP_NIC_DNS="1.1.1.1 2606:4700:4700::1111" \e
    sowa-setup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ,
.Xr sowa-chroot 8 ,
.Xr sowa-pkg 8
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
erases the disk it is given.
It does not create an LVM or mdadm root, an encrypted root, or a disk shared
with another operating system; those layouts are what
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8
is for.
Sowa includes mdadm and LVM2 and activates arrays and volume groups before
mounting fstab entries, but a root placed on either needs a boot arrangement
with an initramfs rather than the direct-root menu this command writes.
.Pp
It must run as root, and on Linux 6.1 or newer.
Every binary in the image was built against glibc configured for that kernel,
and an older one does not fail cleanly - see
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ,
which explains the check the two of them share.
